Recompiler: compile ahead of need from a per-ROM warm list

On a Switch (Cortex-A57) a block costs about 0.5 ms to compile, and the only frames of a match that
miss 16.7 ms are those that compile blocks for the first time (a frame with 68 new blocks: 59 ms).
Every start of the emulator and every newly loaded piece of game code pays that again.

What this adds
- Recording: new_recompile_block notes every block it compiles as (address as it was asked for,
  length of the source in bytes, checksum of the source words), without duplicates.
  new_dynarec_warm_load/read/reset move the list in and out; api/debugger.c exports them as
  DebugDynarecWarmLoad/Read/Reset, with DebugDynarecPrecompile, DebugDynarecWarmStats and
  DebugDynarecWarmConfig (1 verify mode, 2 hash the generated code). A front end keeps the list
  per ROM. It holds no game code.
- Compiling ahead: new_dynarec_warm_precompile(budget_us, oversized_us) compiles listed blocks whose source is in
  RDRAM now (kseg0 addresses; the checksum must be equal), with the length the list gives, until the
  budget is used; a block is only started when the time it should take (a running average per
  instruction, times 1.5) is left. A block that should take longer than a whole budget (the lists
  have about thirty of over 430 instructions, up to 2,728) waits for a call with more: a larger
  budget, or the second argument, the time the caller will give to one such block compiled alone
  in a call. Entries whose source is not there stay pending.
- Cheap when there is nothing to do: entries are indexed by the RDRAM page they start in, and a
  page's entries are looked at only after something that can have brought code there: a DMA into
  the page (invalidate_cached_code_new_dynarec with a size, which the cartridge and disk DMAs call),
  a write to a page that holds compiled code (invalidate_block; also how a rollback load reports
  the pages it changed, patch 0005), a full invalidation (a state load: every page), a compile on
  demand (the block's pages; every page when the list knew the block, since then code arrived
  unseen, copied by the CPU), and one page per call in rotation as a net. With nothing pending the
  call returns at once.

How a block compiled ahead is kept, and why that is safe
A block compiled ahead (a "shadow") goes through the same passes, at `out`, where the next block
would go; then its code is copied to the heap as data and `out` is put back. Pass 10 is not run.
(a) Where the call may be made. In a rollback session the frame callbacks run in r4300_core.c's
    loop after new_dyna_start/new_dyna_resume has returned: new_dyna_stop has unwound the generated
    code and the next frame goes in through get_addr_ht(pcaddr), so no block is on the stack. The
    function does not rely on that, though. It writes: the bytes from `out` on (free space by the
    cache's own rule; the next compile writes there, and the 256K after `out` are what the wrap
    check keeps available), its own heap data, and the compiler's scratch arrays, which are idle
    between two blocks. It does not expire anything, patches no jump of any existing block (no
    add_link, no kill_pointer; set_jump_target only inside the scratch code), frees no list entry
    and flushes nothing. So no block that is going to be resumed, and nothing the rollback code
    relies on, can change under it. It must be called on the emulation thread and not from inside
    a compile.
(b) No emulated state. The compiler reads RDRAM and, for a shadow, leaves alone: the registers,
    hi/lo, cp0, cp1, the cycle count and next_interrupt, pending_exception, the interrupt queue,
    the TLB, memory_map, invalid_code, mini_ht, the hash table, jump_in, jump_dirty, jump_out,
    restore_candidate and the expiry pointer; stop_after_jal is saved and put back (a shadow notes
    that its compile would have set it; the flag is set when the shadow is adopted, which is when a
    compile on demand would have set it). Pages are write protected when a block is adopted, i.e.
    at the moment a compile on demand protects them, not when a shadow is made: a guest write to
    such a page neither traps nor invalidates anything, as without a list, and if it changed the
    block's words the shadow no longer matches. What the guest can time is not touched: the count
    register advances by constants compiled into the code, per instruction, and interrupts are
    taken at the same branches whenever the block was compiled.
(c) The same blocks as without a list. When the game asks for an address, new_recompile_block runs
    pass 1 as always, so the block's extent is decided by what is compiled at that moment
    (check_addr) and by stop_after_jal, exactly as without a list. Only then is a shadow looked for,
    by start address, length, using_tlb and the source words themselves (memcmp). Its code is copied
    to `out` with every pc-relative reference to a place outside the block re-encoded for the new
    address (warm_relocate: b/bl, b.cond, cbz, tbz, ldr literal, adr, adrp; the block's own
    references and its literal pool need no change), and the compile goes on from the linker pass:
    exits are linked to what exists now (check_addr asked with `out` where a compile stands at that
    exit), the entry points go into jump_dirty, jump_in and the hash table in the same order, then
    the common end: cache flush, wrap check, write protection, pass 10. The cache then holds, at the
    same address, what a full compile at that moment would have put there. A run with a list
    therefore compiles the same sequence of blocks, of the same shapes, at the same cache positions
    as a run without one, across cache wraps too, and executes the same code.
    That needs the compiler to be a function of the block alone, which it was not:
    - passes 3 to 7 read regs[] entries not yet written in this compile (register maps of later
      instructions, and the entry after the last instruction), i.e. what the previous compile left
      there: about 2% of blocks got another register allocation depending on the order of compiles.
      They now start from the same state every time.
    - the dirty stubs loaded their list node's address in one of three forms depending on where
      malloc had put the node (arm64/assem_arm64.c), so block sizes and with them the layout of the
      cache differed from run to run. They always use the literal pool now.
    Both change generated host code against the unpatched core (register choice in a few blocks,
    one instruction instead of two in dirty stubs); every RDRAM hash and checksum of the harness is
    unchanged.
    Checked on the Mac: (1) with DebugDynarecWarmConfig(2) the core hashes every block as it enters
    the cache (without its literal pool); with address randomization off that hash is the same
    with and without compiling ahead (znx-bench --no-aslr --code-hash), i.e. the cache receives the
    same code in the same order; (2) in verify mode (config 1) every shadow the game asks for is
    compiled in full as well and compared word for word with the shadow moved to that address:
    none of about 2,900 differ in a 17,000-frame match; (3) the same with all 37 scratch arrays of the compiler
    overwritten with junk before every compile, other junk for shadows than for compiles on demand
    (seven pairs of values): the same code hash each time, 0 differ.
(d) Rollback loads and state loads (patches 0004, 0005). Shadows are in no list, so
    new_dynarec_rollback_note_ram and the invalidations see exactly what they see without them. A
    load only marks pages to be looked at again; a shadow whose source the load changed does not
    match until the code is back. Adopted blocks are ordinary blocks.

Limits: blocks in kseg0 RDRAM only, not those starting in a delay slot or spanning the end of a
mapping (they are recorded, and compiled on demand); moving code is written for ARM64 only, so the
other back ends record but do not compile ahead; at most 64 MB of shadow code is kept. Code that is
loaded and first run within one frame is still compiled in that frame.

